WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.892
A custom column lets you write your own formula.

2
00:00:03.892 --> 00:00:06.906
On the Add Column tab, click Custom Column.

3
00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:10.298
On the right is the list of available columns.

4
00:00:10.298 --> 00:00:14.820
Select a column and click Insert to add it to the formula.

5
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.463
Column names go inside square brackets.

6
00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:35.620
If a bracket is missing, Power Query warns you that a closing bracket is expected.

7
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.206
Here I multiply Units Sold by Manufacturing Price and by Sale Price.

8
00:00:53.206 --> 00:00:59.280
When the message says no syntax errors have been detected, the formula is ready.

9
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.835
I name the column Total Sale and click OK.

10
00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:12.300
The new column is added at the end of the table.

11
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.921
A new custom column often has the type Any, so I set its data

12
00:01:32.921 --> 00:01:34.327
type to Decimal Number.

13
00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:39.600
Then I move Total Sale next to Sale Price, where it is easy to compare.

14
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.562
Every change is saved in Applied Steps.

15
00:02:10.562 --> 00:02:15.686
Right click a step to rename it, delete it, delete until the end, insert

16
00:02:15.686 --> 00:02:18.248
a step after it, or move it.

17
00:02:18.248 --> 00:02:22.640
Give important steps clear names, so your teammates can follow your work.

18
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.709
Each time the data refreshes, Power Query runs these steps again in order, so

19
00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:45.340
your cleaning is repeatable.

